WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:03.235
-Our next guest is a
very funny comedian.

00:00:03.235 --> 00:00:05.338
His international
music collective, "Temps,"

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:08.241
has released their debut single,
"no,no," which is available now.

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:11.811
Please, welcome back to the show
James Acaster, everybody.

00:00:11.811 --> 00:00:14.080
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:29.896
James, I'm so happy to have you
here in person.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:31.797
-Thank you, Seth.
It's good to see you.

00:00:31.797 --> 00:00:34.500
-You're doing a U.S. tour.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.070
-Yeah, yeah, I'm touring the...

00:00:39.138 --> 00:00:40.673
I'm touring the U.S.

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.408
-Got a new show called...
-U.S. of "A."

00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:46.645
-...called -- Your new show is
called "Hecklers Welcome."

00:00:46.645 --> 00:00:48.314
-Guess what the "A" stands for.

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:50.116
-The U.S. of "A"?
-Yes.

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:52.552
-What does the "A"
in U.S. of "A" stand for?

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:54.154
-America.

00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:56.989
You should know, man.
You live here.

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:58.624
That's embarrassing.

00:00:58.624 --> 00:01:00.759
That's some embarrassing stuff.

00:01:00.759 --> 00:01:03.162
I do my research before I talk.

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:04.764
-So, the show is called
"Hecklers Welcome."

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:06.432
A lot of comedians
don't like hecklers,

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:08.300
but it seems like you're just
going to encourage them.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.070
-Oh, I really don't like them.
-Okay. Oh.

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.805
-Yeah, I hate hecklers.
-Okay.

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:14.607
-But I wanted to get over that,

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.142
and this is like
exposure therapy.

00:01:17.142 --> 00:01:18.277
Do you know what that is?

00:01:18.277 --> 00:01:19.779
-Yeah, like if you're
afraid of spiders,

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:21.214
they put them all over you.

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:24.050
-Yeah, like if Macaulay Culkin
stuck his head in a beehive.

00:01:26.285 --> 00:01:29.455
-Was that what that was?
That was exposure therapy?

00:01:29.455 --> 00:01:31.391
-That's what that
whole film's about.

00:01:32.791 --> 00:01:33.893
-I had no idea.

00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:35.194
Changes it.

00:01:35.194 --> 00:01:37.063
-Yeah, yeah, he's scared of
loads of stuff, that kid.

00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:40.366
Burglars.
He's scared of burglars.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:42.669
-Oh, did he invite
the burglars to come

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:44.504
as a way of getting over
his fear of burglars?

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:46.806
-They cut that scene from
the film where

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.774
his therapist tells him,

00:01:48.774 --> 00:01:52.578
"Why don't you just try exposing
yourself to more burglars."

00:01:52.578 --> 00:01:53.879
That was what
the whole thing was about.

00:01:53.879 --> 00:01:56.482
-Wow! I feel bad for
whoever played the therapist.

00:01:56.482 --> 00:01:58.317
Obviously, that was a pretty
juicy part, and then --

00:01:58.317 --> 00:01:59.618
-Yeah, yeah.
That was pretty bad.

00:01:59.618 --> 00:02:01.321
But that was -- You know who
played the therapist?

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:04.390
-Who was that?
-David Pumpkins.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.325
-The "SNL" character?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:06.325 --> 00:02:08.360
That was -- He got his break
further down the line

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.896
when he got on "SNL."

00:02:10.896 --> 00:02:13.933
But it was originally
David S. Pumpkins.

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:18.404
-You have a musical collective
called Temps.

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:21.140
-Guilty.
-And you have a new song out.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.909
And this is from
the video of the song,

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:27.313
and this is
an alligator suit of sorts.

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.281
And this is your alligator suit.

00:02:29.281 --> 00:02:30.816
-Yes, I had that
made for myself,

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:32.452
and I'm in that alligator suit.

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:35.054
-Gotcha.
-And I got heatstroke.

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:36.522
-Really?

00:02:36.522 --> 00:02:40.226
Do you blame it on the suit
or the day you filmed the video

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.761
or a combination?

00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:44.763
-A combination.
Also my own stupidity.

00:02:44.763 --> 00:02:47.399
-Okay.
-It wasn't a good idea.

00:02:47.399 --> 00:02:49.068
I wouldn't advise it to anyone.

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:52.204
But I got myself an
alligator mascot suit made,

00:02:52.204 --> 00:02:54.740
and for -- I might as well have
gotten on the roller coasters.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.142
Actually, that was day two.
Day one, I got heatstroke.

00:02:57.142 --> 00:02:58.945
-Well, you really -- I mean,
these seem like awful things

00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:01.947
to be doing in an alligator suit
on a hot day.

00:03:01.947 --> 00:03:03.049
What was your vision?

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:05.084
Why did you want to make
an alligator suit?

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:07.119
-Well, I had
a toy like that as a kid,

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:08.787
a massive party gator.

00:03:08.787 --> 00:03:10.689
-Uh-huh.
-T-shirt says, "Party gator."

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:12.258
I won it at a fair.
-Uh-huh.

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:14.627
-Got it as a kid at
the same time I got my drum kit.

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:17.964
Then, as an adult, made this
album, got the drum kit,

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:20.900
got the party gator
back from my parents' house.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.036
It was meant to be a big thing,
and then, when COVID hit,

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:26.439
I gave both of them, the
drum kit and the party gator,

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:28.474
to a school I used to work at
for safekeeping.

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:29.541
-Uh-huh.

00:03:29.541 --> 00:03:30.843
-Then, for a video, I was like,

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:31.844
"I'm going to put that
toy in the video."

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:33.145
Phoned the school up.

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:35.348
They were like, "Yeah,
we threw that in the trash."

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:38.951
"Oh, that was my childhood toy."

00:03:38.951 --> 00:03:40.953
They're like, "Yeah. Thanks."

00:03:40.953 --> 00:03:43.822
And --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.822 --> 00:03:46.258
So then I had to
get that made myself.

00:03:46.258 --> 00:03:47.726
Obviously,
that's what I had to do.

00:03:47.726 --> 00:03:50.029
I was like, "I guess I've become
the party gator now."

00:03:50.029 --> 00:03:51.764
-Well, there was something --
You used to work at

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:52.898
the amusement park, right?

00:03:52.898 --> 00:03:54.333
-I used to work there.

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:55.935
I used to serve food and drink
at that amusement park.

00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:56.969
That's how they let me in now.

00:03:56.969 --> 00:03:58.104
They give me
the run of the place.

00:03:58.104 --> 00:03:59.338
-Oh, wow.

00:03:59.338 --> 00:04:01.440
So if you work at an
amusement park in England,

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.543
you just sort of -- Forever,
the rest of your life, you're --

00:04:03.543 --> 00:04:05.211
-You phone them up and go, "I'm
dressed as a party gator today.

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:06.412
Can I come in?

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:08.748
They're like,
"You don't even need to ask."

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:11.316
-They did an even nicer thing.

00:04:11.316 --> 00:04:15.288
They named the goat at
the amusement park after you.

00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:17.289
-Yep, that goat is called
James Acaster.

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:20.192
-Wow! Did you ever think,
when your career started,

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:21.627
it would hit that height?

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:26.465
-Well, I always hoped that,
one day, I'd be the GOAT.

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:28.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.071
-You did it.
-I did it, man.

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:33.539
-You did it. Pretty early.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:35.307
And now who knows
what height you're going to hit.

00:04:35.307 --> 00:04:36.676
Already the GOAT.

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:39.378
-Yeah, I can't wait to see
what dizzy heights I get to.

00:04:39.378 --> 00:04:42.582
Here we are at 30 Rock.
It means a lot, man.

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:48.254
This is where I first heard of
David S. Pumpkins.

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:50.723
-Right, at 30 Rock.
-30 Rock. And now I'm here.

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:52.525
-And then --
You first heard of him here,

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:55.327
then you IMDB'd him, saw that he
was cut from "Home Alone."

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:57.229
-I was like, "Oh, I bet there's
an interesting story

00:04:57.229 --> 00:04:58.964
behind that."

00:04:58.964 --> 00:05:02.435
-You also -- So, you're a
music producer, a stand-up,

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:04.236
You have a true-crime podcast.

00:05:04.236 --> 00:05:05.771
-Yeah, I've got the
true-crime podcast coming out.

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:08.407
What a lot people
don't know about me is that --

00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:09.509
Yeah, you know me as
a stand-up comedian.

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:10.643
-I do.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:12.178
-But I'm actually
an undercover cop

00:05:12.178 --> 00:05:13.613
who's undercover
as a stand-up comedian.

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:16.816
-Oh. From the beginning?
-From the very beginning.

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.717
I had to infiltrate
a gang of drug dealers

00:05:18.717 --> 00:05:20.118
who were dealing drugs
to comedians

00:05:20.118 --> 00:05:22.421
backstage at comedy gigs.

00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:25.290
So I became a stand-up comedian
to infiltrate the gang.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:26.759
The case didn't go great,

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:29.428
but my comedy career has gone
from strength to strength.

00:05:29.428 --> 00:05:32.231
-Oh.
-That's just what happened.

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:34.934
And I've decided, now that I've
been kicked out the force,

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:36.602
I'll just do
a true-crime podcast

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:38.704
where I share my wire recordings
with the public.

00:05:38.704 --> 00:05:40.672
-Oh, wow.
-Yeah. So that's what it is.

00:05:40.672 --> 00:05:41.907
It's called "Springleaf."

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:43.342
We've got
a Kickstarter for it now,

00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:45.811
because I ain't paying for it.

00:05:45.811 --> 00:05:47.713
-Why should you?
-Why should I pay for it?

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:51.183
I'm on an undercover cop.
You see the wages we're on?

00:05:51.183 --> 00:05:53.919
-Is it better or worse wages
when you go undercover?

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:56.355
Do you get a per diem
when you go undercover?

00:05:56.355 --> 00:05:57.623
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
You get a per diem.

00:05:57.623 --> 00:05:59.292
They go like,
"You're being undercover today,

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:00.526
so you have this."

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:03.462
And what I --
The mistake I make is that I --

00:06:03.462 --> 00:06:05.831
In front of the drug dealers,
I pull out my per diems

00:06:05.831 --> 00:06:08.901
in the envelope that says
"Undercover-cop per diems."

00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:12.872
"Now, what's that?"
I'm like, "Oh, just a joke."

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:16.175
-Oh, well, that's a good cover.
-Comedian -- they love it.

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:19.679
-You also have a self-help book.

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:20.746
-Yeah.

00:06:20.746 --> 00:06:22.882
-Based on something
you went through?

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:24.550
-What people don't know
about me, Seth --

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:27.487
-Uh-huh. Is most of it.

00:06:30.055 --> 00:06:32.591
-Yeah, I'm a party gator.

00:06:32.591 --> 00:06:34.660
I'm a musician.

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.595
I'm a comedian.
I'm an undercover cop.

00:06:36.595 --> 00:06:39.966
I'm also a self-help guru.
-Okay.

00:06:39.966 --> 00:06:41.333
-I came off of social media.

00:06:41.333 --> 00:06:42.634
Everyone wanted to know
how I did it.

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:44.136
So I've released
"James Acaster's

00:06:44.136 --> 00:06:46.005
Guide to Quitting Social Media:
Being the Best You You Can Be

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:47.907
and Curing Yourself
of Loneliness -- Volume 1."

00:06:47.907 --> 00:06:49.742
-Okay.

00:06:49.742 --> 00:06:52.344
-A lot of tips in there
about how to quit social media.

00:06:52.344 --> 00:06:54.880
The main important thing is,
you have to take what

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:56.815
social media used to
give you and you have to

00:06:56.815 --> 00:06:58.417
replicate that
in your day-to-day life.

00:06:58.417 --> 00:06:59.485
-Oh, interesting.

00:06:59.485 --> 00:07:01.053
-So, now, you know,
in normal life,

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:04.824
I have to stalk my girlfriends
for real -- my ex-girlfriends.

00:07:04.824 --> 00:07:07.626
I start arguments
with strangers all the time.

00:07:07.626 --> 00:07:10.863
-Just on the street?
-Really aggressive arguments.

00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:14.000
Saying stuff that I should not
be saying, quite frankly.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.102
-Yeah.

00:07:16.102 --> 00:07:18.203
-Yeah, I do all of that,
and if you want to get

00:07:18.203 --> 00:07:19.638
updates on
all your friends' kids,

00:07:19.638 --> 00:07:23.108
you got to do what I did
and open your own crèche.

00:07:23.108 --> 00:07:24.676
Do you even call it
a crèche in this country?

00:07:24.676 --> 00:07:28.047
I got the big sense just then
that you don't.

00:07:30.482 --> 00:07:32.518
Listen, when I did
my research about America,

00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:35.354
I learned what the "A" stood for
and I stopped there.

00:07:37.122 --> 00:07:40.626
I didn't bother looking up
if you had crèches or not.

00:07:40.626 --> 00:07:43.362
-There was a real -- You felt
the wind when you said "crèche."

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:45.931
-Oh, I felt it. I felt it hard.

00:07:45.931 --> 00:07:48.734
-What do you think -- I feel bad
that I haven't even --

00:07:48.734 --> 00:07:51.904
Based on what you said, I still
don't know what a crèche is.

00:07:51.904 --> 00:07:52.938
How would you describe it?

00:07:52.938 --> 00:07:54.140
I'll try to find
the word for it.

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:55.374
-Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:07:55.374 --> 00:07:57.009
It's like where people go
and drop their kids off

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.110
for the day to be looked after.

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.512
-Like a daycare.

00:08:00.512 --> 00:08:02.548
-Yeah, if you want to say that.

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:08.654
-Well, now you know.

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:09.822
You're going to do
your show here in New York City.

00:08:09.822 --> 00:08:11.023
You're doing stand-up here.

00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:12.324
-Yeah, I'm doing stand-up here
in New York City.

00:08:12.324 --> 00:08:13.692
I'm going to yell out,

00:08:13.692 --> 00:08:15.728
"Hey, give me a cheer
if you came from the crèche!

00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:18.497
Oh, no [bleep]
Daycare! Daycare!"

00:08:18.497 --> 00:08:21.467
-And then think of me
and how I bailed you out.

00:08:21.467 --> 00:08:24.636
-Yeah, I'll be like, "That joke
goes out to Seth Meyers."

00:08:24.636 --> 00:08:26.939
Actually, I'm gonna dedicate
every joke tonight to you, Seth.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:28.240
And I won't even
tell the audience why,

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.244
but I will say it
after every single gag.

00:08:32.511 --> 00:08:35.114
-I have really enjoyed
having you here.

00:08:35.114 --> 00:08:36.415
Thank you so much
for making time.

00:08:36.415 --> 00:08:38.217
Please come back soon.
Have a great tour.

00:08:38.217 --> 00:08:39.585
-It's been a pleasure, Seth.

00:08:39.585 --> 00:08:41.420
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:42.588
-James Acaster, everybody.

00:08:42.588 --> 00:08:44.223
"no,no" is out now.
We'll be right back.

